    "q-79854": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79854",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Solve the equation ${log}_{6}⁡(x+3)+{log}_{6}⁡(2-x)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The logarithms on the left-hand side share the same base, so we can combine them using the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡(x+3)+{log}_{6}⁡(2-x) & =1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡[(x+3)(2-x)] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(2-x) & ={6}^{1} \\\\ 2x-{x}^{2}+6-3x & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $x=-1.$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting them back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡(0+3)+{log}_{6}⁡(2-0) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(3)+{log}_{6}⁡(2) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(3\\cdot 2) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(6) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡(-1+3)+{log}_{6}⁡(2-(-1)) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(2)+{log}_{6}⁡(3) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(2\\cdot 3) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡(6) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=0$ and $x=-1$\".",
